title start
kind: default
tags: shell tui
Plan: the aiu-shell TUI
title end

section start
title start
Section 1: decided
title end

paragraph start
The shell gets its own TUI. aiu-shell owns the screen and draws every box itself. It works alone and does not need the multiplexer; the multiplexer can host and combine it.
paragraph end

paragraph start
Every command makes a pair of boxes: a command box holding what was typed and an output box holding what it printed. Both have a title edge and side bars on every row.
paragraph end

paragraph start
The shell starts in visual mode, not insert mode, because most of the time he goes back through history and boxes. A key enters insert mode to type, and escape returns to visual mode.
paragraph end

paragraph start
Navigation between boxes uses the Dvorak keys: d and n move left and right, h and t move down and up.
paragraph end

paragraph start
A key selects the current box. Selected boxes can be copied: the command box alone, the output box alone, or the pair. Copy goes to the clipboard, so a command given by the assistant and its output can be separated and pasted back without selecting text by hand.
paragraph end

paragraph start
It is built piece by piece. Each piece is run and checked on screen before the next piece starts.
paragraph end

section end

section start
title start
Section 2: what exists now
title end

paragraph start
The shell crate aiu-shell-tui already holds ratatui and crossterm based TUI commands: tui run owns the terminal and returns one record. It is a possible base for the boxes.
paragraph end

paragraph start
The REPL loop is in aiu-shell-cli repl.rs: evaluate_repl and loop_iteration run the line editor reedline once per command. The reedline box patch is a stopgap and is dropped when the own TUI exists.
paragraph end

section end

section start
title start
Section 3: pieces in order
title end

paragraph start
Piece 1: the screen. Boxes drawn with fixed text, title edges, side bars, resize, no shell inside yet.
paragraph end

paragraph start
Piece 2: the input box with modes. Visual mode is the start, insert mode types, escape goes back, history is browsed in visual mode.
paragraph end

paragraph start
Piece 3: running a command. Input goes to the shell engine, the output is captured and drawn in a new output box under its command box.
paragraph end

paragraph start
Piece 4: box navigation and selection with d n h t and a select key.
paragraph end

paragraph start
Piece 5: copy keys for the command box, the output box and the pair.
paragraph end

paragraph start
Piece 6: scrolling inside long output boxes and a key to fold or open a box.
paragraph end

paragraph start
Piece 7: programs that need a real terminal, such as editors and pagers, run in a pty and take the screen until they end.
paragraph end

section end

section start
title start
Section 4: still open
title end

paragraph start
Which language the TUI layer is written in, and whether it is built on the existing aiu-shell-tui crate or draws its own cells.
paragraph end

paragraph start
How copy reaches the clipboard on his phone setup, inside Termux and proot.
paragraph end

paragraph start
How the multiplexer hosts the shell TUI and how the two divide the screen when run together.
paragraph end

paragraph start
How stderr is shown and how exit code and time appear on the output box edge.
paragraph end

paragraph start
How the boxes are saved, so history browsing after a restart brings back whole pairs of boxes.
paragraph end

section end


section start
title start
Section 4: piece 1 built
title end

paragraph start
Piece 1 lives in the new crate aiu-shell/shell/crates/aiu-shell-screen (ratatui and crossterm, own draw code, no engine). The old crate aiu-shell-tui is a pipeline command set with a 3038 line session and is not the base; it stays untouched. The binary aiu-shell-screen with the argument dump prints the screen headless; without an argument it opens the screen and Esc quits. Fixed text only, nothing runs inside.
paragraph end

section end

section start
title start
Section 5: piece 2 built
title end

paragraph start
Piece 2 lives in aiu-shell-screen: input_state.rs holds the modes and history as a pure state machine with tests, screen_draw.rs draws the pairs above and the input box below. Visual mode is the start: t older history, h newer history, i insert, q quit. Insert mode: typing, backspace, enter keeps the line as a new pair with the output not run yet, escape back to visual. Nothing runs yet; piece 3 runs the command.
paragraph end

section end

section start
title start
Section 6: piece 3 built
title end

paragraph start
Piece 3 lives in aiu-shell-screen: command_runner.rs starts aiu-shell with no config file and -c for the line, captures stdout and stderr, strips escape codes, cuts at 200 rows and appends the exit code. InputState new_running makes Enter in insert mode run the line and fill the output box. The binary argument run with a line prints the result headless. A fresh process per line is the first step; one kept engine comes next.
paragraph end

section end

section start
title start
Section 7: piece 4 built
title end

paragraph start
Piece 4 lives in aiu-shell-screen: input_state.rs has Mode Boxes with focus (box index, pair times two plus side) and marked boxes; b enters from visual mode, t and h move by pair, d and n switch between command box and output box, s marks or unmarks, escape returns and keeps the marks; screen_draw.rs draws the focused box yellow and bold and marked boxes magenta.
paragraph end

section end

section start
title start
Section 8: history view built
title end

paragraph start
The history view lives in aiu-shell-screen: history_view.rs reads the aiu-shell-history database with rusqlite (newest first, unique commands, exit mark, age, folder), filters while typing (substring matches first, then fuzzy), and screen_draw.rs draws it above the input box with the best match at the bottom. Slash in visual mode and ctrl+r in insert or boxes mode open it, up and down move, enter puts the command into the input box, escape closes and restores the typed text.
paragraph end

section end

section start
title start
Section 9: copy mode built
title end

paragraph start
Piece 5 lives in aiu-shell-screen: in boxes mode c copies the focused box, p copies the pair and a copies the marked boxes. clipboard_copy.rs writes the OSC 52 escape with its own base64 and also pipes the text to termux-clipboard-set when it exists. The title shows how many characters were copied.
paragraph end

section end
